A FastAPI path operation can return both JSON and an image based on the request parameters by using the `responses` parameter in the path operation decorator. Specifically, you can define different media types for the same main response. For example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI
from fastapi.responses import FileResponse
from pydantic import BaseModel


class Item(BaseModel):
    id: str
    value: str


app = FastAPI()


@app.get(
    "/items/{item_id}",
    response_model=Item,
    responses={
        200: {
            "content": {"image/png": {}},
            "description": "Return the JSON item or an image.",
        }
    },
)
async def read_item(item_id: str, img: bool | None = None):
    if img:
        return FileResponse("image.png", media_type="image/png")
    else:
        return {"id": "foo", "value": "there goes my hero"}
```

In this example, the path operation `/items/{item_id}` can return either a JSON object or an image based on the `img` query parameter. If `img` is `True`, it returns an image; otherwise, it returns a JSON object.

When you encounter issues with API documentation, you can use `pydantic.dataclasses` instead of the standard `dataclasses`. Specifically, you can replace the standard `dataclasses` import with `pydantic.dataclasses` as a drop-in replacement. Here is an example from the provided documentation [3]:

```python
from dataclasses import field  # (1)

from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic.dataclasses import dataclass  # (2)


@dataclass
class Item:
    name: str
    description: str | None = None


@dataclass
class Author:
    name: str
    items: list[Item] = field(default_factory=list)  # (3)


app = FastAPI()


@app.post("/authors/{author_id}/items/", response_model=Author)  # (4)
async def create_author_items(author_id: str, items: list[Item]):  # (5)
    return {"name": author_id, "items": items}  # (6)


@app.get("/authors/", response_model=list[Author])  # (7)
def get_authors():  # (8)
    return [  # (9)
        {
            "name": "Breaters",
            "items": [
                {
                    "name": "Island In The Moon",
                    "description": "A place to be playin' and havin' fun",
                },
                {"name": "Holy Buddies"},
            ],
        },
        {
            "name": "System of an Up",
            "items": [
                {
                    "name": "Salt",
                    "description": "The kombucha mushroom people's favorite",
                },
                {"name": "Pad Thai"},
                {
                    "name": "Lonely Night",
                    "description": "The mostests lonliest nightiest of allest",
                },
            ],
        },
    ]
```

In this example, the `pydantic.dataclasses` import is used as a drop-in replacement for the standard `dataclasses` [3]. This can help resolve issues with the automatically generated API documentation.

A FastAPI developer can handle different HTTP status codes based on the existence of data and the need to create new data by using a `Response` object to set the appropriate status code. This can be done in a path operation function as shown in the example from passage [3]:

```python
from fastapi import Body, FastAPI, status
from fastapi.responses import JSONResponse

app = FastAPI()

items = {"foo": {"name": "Fighters", "size": 6}, "bar": {"name": "Tenders", "size": 3}}


@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
async def upsert_item(
    item_id: str,
    name: Annotated[str | None, Body()] = None,
    size: Annotated[int | None, Body()] = None,
):
    if item_id in items:
        item = items[item_id]
        item["name"] = name
        item["size"] = size
        return item
    else:
        item = {"name": name, "size": size}
        items[item_id] = item
        return JSONResponse(status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED, content=item)
```

In this example, if the item exists, it updates the item and returns a 200 "OK" status. If the item does not exist, it creates a new item and returns a 201 "Created" status [3].

